 ## Overview
-The Depth Distribution Constraint is a regularization term applied during the optimization of 3D Gaussian splatting to constrain Gaussian points to aggregate at object surfaces, thereby improving 3D structural reconstruction quality [S1, S2]. It minimizes depth uncertainty by penalizing the variance of the depth distribution along rays, driving Gaussians toward the true surface [S2]. It is distinct from the sibling [Depth Distortion Loss](9667-depth-distortion-loss.md) by explicitly formulating the penalty as the difference between the second moment of depth and the square of the first moment [S2].
+The Depth Distribution Constraint is a regularization term applied during 3D Gaussian splatting optimization to constrain Gaussians to aggregate at object surfaces, improving 3D structural reconstruction quality [S1, S2]. It minimizes depth uncertainty by penalizing the variance of the depth distribution along rays, driving Gaussians toward the true surface [S2]. It is distinct from the sibling [Depth Distortion Loss](9667-depth-distortion-loss.md) by explicitly formulating the penalty as the difference between the second moment of depth and the square of the first moment [S2].
 
