 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ per ray, where $N$ is the number of Gaussians along the ray. This is negligible compared to the rendering and densification steps.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ additional memory, as it only requires summing two values.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ per ray, where $N$ is the number of Gaussians along the ray [G]. This is negligible compared to the rendering and densification steps [G].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ additional memory, as it only requires summing two values [G].
 *   **Strengths:** Improves 3D structural reconstruction, which is crucial for downstream tasks like refocusing [S1]. It effectively reduces foreground/background ambiguity by forcing Gaussians to the surface [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the assumption that the object possesses an accurate surface [S2]. It may not be suitable for transparent or volumetric scenes where depth is inherently distributed.
-*   **When to use:** When structural fidelity is important for downstream applications (e.g., refocusing, normal estimation) and the scene consists of opaque surfaces.
-*   **When not to use:** For scenes with significant transparency or volumetric effects where depth is not concentrated at a single surface.
